</exclusiva>

  <h3>Description of the parameters file (*.MMC) and the mathematical and logical
  syntax:</h3>(you need not to read it when using the windows interface)<br/><br/>
  <ul>
    <li><b>[Formula]:</b> Section which contains information on the
    mathematical formula which is to be calculated.<br/>
    <b>Expressio:</b> String that represents the logical-mathematical
    operation to be executed. It is composed of numbers, images, operands and
    functions. Image names are aliases of the corresponding files and are
    given between claudators ('[' and ']'). Implemented
    operands and functions are described below. Function arguments need to be
    within parenthesis; e.g: SING(30). If the function contains more than
    argument these will be separated by commas; e.g: MAX([IMG1],[IMG2]). When
    not sure it is recommended to use the syntax that is constructed by
    clicking onto the corresponding buttons and menus of the Windows
    application without editing them, or at most, adding the needed
    parameters<br/></li>
  </ul>

  <ul>
    <li><b>[Result]:</b> Characteristics of the resulting file of the
    mathematical expression.<br/>
    <b>Fitxer:</b> Name of the resulting file. Its path is relative to the
    location of the parameters file.<br/>
    <b>Tipus:</b> Data type of the resulting file (auto, byte, byte-RLE,
    etc.). Automatic means that the program has adjusted the final data type
    with respect to the range and integer or real data type of the values of
    the resulting file and of its degree of compression. Automatic will not
    compress when at least one of the images in the expression is compressed,
    except for the case of the resulting image being of 0 and 1 values. In
    this case, the resulting image will be byte-RLE. Bit data are generated
    only when specified because, usually, a byte-RLE format will result in
    files smaller in size.<br/>
    <b>Titol:</b> Descriptive title of the resulting image. When left blank,
    by default, the title will be the mathematical formula.<br/>
    <b>Desc_valors:</b>Descriptive string of the type of values that will
    result.<br/>
    <b>Fitxer_categories:</b>In the case of categorical results, it is the IMG
    file from which categories are imported. Its address is relative to the
    parameters file.<br/>
    <b>Valor_Fons:</b> Value given to the flag value or &quot;NODATA&quot; in
    the resulting file. The literal &quot;NODATA&quot; indicates that the
    program itself will decide which value will be chosen.<br/>
    <b>Stretch:</b>0 means that no stretching will be applied and that values
    beyond the range of the chosen domain will be saturated. 1 means that
    values are linearly scalated to the range in-between the minimum and
    maximum stretch. 0 is the default value, which corresponds to
    saturation.<br/>
    <b>Minim_Stretch:</b> It is activated with Stretch=1. X is the default
    value according to the chosen data type.<br/>
    <b>Maxim_Stretch:</b> It is activated with Stretch=1. X is the default
    value according to the chosen data type.<br/>
    <b>Truncar:</b>If real values are not chosen, any resulting image will be
    rounded when Truncar=0 or will be truncated when Truncar=1. 0 is the
    default value.<br/>
    <b>Reservar_V_Fons:</b>0 implies that no value for NODATA is reserved from
    start. 1 implies that a value is reserved for possible NODATA values in
    the result. Its default value is 0. <b>X_minima, X_maxima, Y_minima,
    Y_maxima:</b> Definition of the geographical extent of the resulting
    raster when there is no raster in the mathematical expression.<br/>
    <b>Costat_pixel:</b>Pixel size of the resulting raster when there is no
    raster in the mathematical expression.<br/></li>
  </ul>

  <ul>
    <li><b>[Variables]: </b>In this section the correspondences between the
    aliases of the images that appear in the formula and the actual files are
    specified. Paths are relative to the location of the parameters
    file.<br/>
  </ul>

  <ul>
    <li><b>[Reclassificadors]: </b>This is the section where the aliases that
    appear in the formula for the reclassification files are related to their
    full real names. The paths of these files are relative to the parameters
    file.</li>

    <li><b>[Alias_vector]:</b> Each vector in the [Variables] section will
    have its own section named after its alias and with the following
    contents:<br/>
    <b>base:</b> For topologically-structured vectors, -1 when referred to the
    main table or the index of the field with which the relation with the
    associated table is established (starting from 0).<br/>
    <b>camp:</b> For topologically-structured vectors, the index on which
    calculations are performed (indexed from 0).<br/>
    <b>repeticio:</b> For topologically-structured vectors with multiple
    records, it is the index of the record to be taken into account: 1,2..
    (starting from the first record), -1,-2... (starting from the last
    record).<br/>
    <b>valor_exterior:</b> Value that will take the emulated raster, external
    to the vector and inside the defined geographical extent.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Implemented operands and functions</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Arithmetical operators:</b></li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
+   Sum or positive sign.
-      Substraction or negative sign.
*      Multiplication.
/      Division.
</pre>
  </blockquote>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Logical and Comparison operators (when true the result is 1, when
    false the result is 0):</b></li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
AND    I. 
OR     Or.
NOT    Negation
XOR    Exclusive Or.
&gt;      Strictly bigger.
&gt;=     Equal or bigger.
&lt;      Strictly smaller.
&lt;=     Equal or smaller. 
=      Equal.
=/=    Different.
</pre>
  </blockquote>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Mathematical functions of only one argument:</b></li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
SQRT   Square root.
LOG    Base 10 logarithm.
LN     Natural logarithm.
EXP    Exponential.
INV    Inverse.
ENT    Truncation.
ABS    Absolute value.
RND    Random value between 0 and the argument. 

</pre>
  </blockquote>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Logical functions of only one argument:</b></li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
BOL    Boolean.
</pre>
  </blockquote>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Mathematical functions of two arguments:</b></li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
POW    Power. 
NR     Normalized ratio.
MOD    Division remainder.
</pre>
  </blockquote>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Comparison functions of two arguments:</b></li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
MAX    the Bigger of two values.
MIN    the Smaller of two values.
</pre>
  </blockquote>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Reclassification functions and category grouping:</b></li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
RECL   Reclassification.
</pre>
  </blockquote>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Layer overlay functions:</b></li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
CVR    Transparent (*).
</pre>
  </blockquote>(*) Transparent function (also named <i>Cover</i>) returns the
  value of the second argument when the first argument is equal to the flag
  value (value defined by NODATA in the documentation of the first file in
  case of rasters) and returns the first argument when it is different to this
  value. This way, values of the second file can be seen through NODATA zones
  of the first layer. As in the other cases, any of the arguments of
  Transparent can be the partial results of other operations between layers
  and scalars.

  <ul>
    <li><b>Trigonometric functions with the angle expressed in
    radiants:</b></li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
SIN    Sine.
COS    Cosine.
TAN    Tangent.
ASIN   Arc sine.
ACOS   Arc cosine.
ATAN   Arc tangent.
COSEC  Cosecant.
SEC    Secant.
CTG    Cotangent.
</pre>
  </blockquote>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Trigonometric functions with the angle expressed in
    degrees:</b></li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
SING   Sine.
COSG   Cosine.
TANG   Tangent.
ASING  Arc sine.
ACOSG  Arc cosine.
ATANG  Arc tangent.
COSECG Cosecant.
SECG   Secant.
CTGG   Cotangent.
</pre>
  </blockquote>

<p>The order of precedence is governed by the six categories of
  operands and functions ordered from bigger to smaller precedence. Within the
  same category the operations are carried out from left to right. The
  different categories are:<br/>
  <br/></p>

  <ul>
    <li>1: ()</li>

    <li>2: + , - [as positive and negative signs] and functions of only one
    argument [e.g.: SIN, LOG...]</li>

    <li>3: Functions of two arguments [e.g.: POW, CVR, MAX, ...]</li>

    <li>4: * , /</li>

    <li>5: + , - [as sum and substraction operations]</li>

    <li>6: logical [p. ex.: AND, &gt;=...]<br/>
    <br/></li>
  </ul>
<p>The conditional function ? has not been implemented (condition,
  function_TRUE, function_FALSE) since it can be simulated with a combination
  of logical operations in the form:
  <pre>
(condition)*(function_TRUE)+(NOT condition)*(function_FALSE) 
</pre>

  <h3>Some examples</h3><b>Example 1:</b> We want to create an image as if it
  had already been multiplied by a mask: Suppose we want to select from image
  'A' all zones which have values bigger than 3000 in image
  'B'.<br/>
  The operation will be: <font face="Courier New" size="-1">[A]*([B]&gt;3000)</font><br/>
  In this case, the resulting image will have the value 0 in all zones with
  values that are equal or less than 3000 in image 'B', and will
  maintain the original values in 'A' in the rest of the image (zones
  with values greater than 3000).<br/>
  <br/>
  <b>Exemple 2:</b> We want to reclassify the values of an image 'C'
  in two groups. All values that are less than 100 will become 50 and values
  equal or greater than 100 will become 150.<br/>
  The operation will be: <font face="Courier New" size="-1">([C]&lt;100)*50+(NOT([C]&lt;100))*150.</font><br/>
  Alternatively, the following form can be used: <font face="Courier New" size="-1">([C]&lt;100)*50+([C]&gt;=100)*150.</font><br/>
  The resulting image will have two different values: 50 for those that where
  smaller than 100 and 150 for those that where greater than 100.<br/>
  The function of reclassification can also be used: <font face="Courier New" size="-1">RECL([MMReclass],[C])</font> if we do know the range of values in
  the raster 'C'. If 'C' is a raster of type byte or byte-RLE
  the contents of the reclassification file MMReclass.MRC would be:

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
0 100 50
100 256 150 
</pre>
  </blockquote>or, if we do not know the range of values for raster
  'C', a reclassification based in a MRC file such as the following is
  also admitted:<br/>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
-1.0E+40 100 50
100 1.0E+40 150
</pre>
  </blockquote>

  <p><br/>
  <b>Example 3:</b> Suppose we want to obtain a raster file with the following
  grouped categories:</p>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
category  1 : Dense forest                 
category  2 : Sparse forest                 
category  3 : Riparian forests            
category  4 : Reforestations
category  5 : Poplar plantations                
category  6 : Platanus plantations                  
category  7 : Shrublands                   
category  8 : Wetlands
category  9 : Meadows          
category 10 : CORINE Fires
category 11 : Rock outcrops
category 12 : Scree
category 13 : Bare soil                  
category 14 : Beaches
category 15 : Continental waters         
category 16 : Glaciers and permanent snow
category 17 : Cultivation fields
category 18 : Urban zones
category 19 : Roads   
category 20 : Sport zones
category 21 : Quarrying zones
</pre>
  </blockquote>according to the reclassification:

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
1 11 1
11 17 2
17 3
18 21 4
</pre>
  </blockquote>The resulting raster will have four categories which could be
  defined as:

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
category  1 : Forests
category  2 : Non-productive natural zones
category  3 : Cultivation fields
category  4 : Non-productive artificial zones
</pre>
  </blockquote>

  <p><br/>
  <b>Example 4:</b> Given an image, 256c, if you want to assign value 1 to all
  values that are equal to &quot;3&quot; or to &quot;12&quot; and
  &quot;0&quot; to the rest of the values, then you will write:</p>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
([256c]=3)OR([256c]=12)
</pre>
  </blockquote>This operation can also be done from within MiraMon through a
  query by attributes and saving the file as a IMG file.

  <p><br/>
  <b>Example 5:</b> Given an image, 256c, in order to select both values 3 and
  12, but preserving these values in the result and making the rest of the
  values as 0, we will write:<br/></p>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
(([256c]=3)OR([256c]=12))*[256c]
</pre>
  </blockquote><br/>
  <b>Example 6:</b> How to obtain the digital elevation model from three
  States out of the digital elevation model of a of USA '[USA_DEM]'
  and the vector layer of the counties '[States]'.

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
[USA_DEM]*([States;State;]=&quot;Connecticut&quot; OR [States;State;]=&quot;Wisconsin&quot;
OR [States;State;]=&quot;Idaho&quot;)
</pre>
  </blockquote>It should be noted that the resulting file will have the same
  extent as [USA_DEM], the whole USA, and the <a href="Retalla.htm">Retalla</a> application needs to be used in order to reduce
  the extent.

  <p><br/>
  <b>Example 7:</b> The rasterization of an arc layer, '[Profiles]',
  converting rasterized lines to 0 value and the external zone to 1 is a less
  intuitive but interesting example that shows how dynamic rasterization is
  treated inside the calculator.</p>

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
[Profiles;LONG_ARC;]=0
where we have defined 'Value external to the vector'=0
</pre>
  </blockquote>There is no arc with longitude equal to 0 in the cells affected
  by the vector (rasterized) and, thus, the logical operation returns FALSE,
  that is 0. On the other hand, as the external zone has been assigned the
  value 0, which compared to value 0, the result is TRUE, that is 1.
  
   <p><br/> 
   <b>Example 8:</b> We need to define a random value between -1 and 1. Raster1 is a raster file with the constant value 1, is necessary to define the extent and the pixel size of the output file.

  <blockquote>
    <pre>
(RND(2)-1)*[Raster1]
	</pre>
	 </blockquote>
  
  <hr/>

</body>
</html>